# Page Container/Component Architecture

Each page lives in its own directory under `src/pages/`. Split UI from logic using `components/` and `container/`.

## Directory Structure

```
src/pages/{scope}/{page-name}/
  index.ts              # Re-exports container as default (route entry point)
  container/
    index.ts            # Re-exports container
    {Page}Container.tsx # Logic: handlers, validation, API calls, state
  components/
    index.ts            # Re-exports UI components
    {Page}.tsx          # Pure UI — no hooks, API calls, or business logic
```

## Responsibilities

| Layer | Owns | Must NOT own |
|-------|------|--------------|
| **Component** | JSX, layout, styling, rendering props | `useState`, `useEffect`, API calls, validation, Redux dispatch |
| **Container** | State, effects, handlers (`handleChange`, `handleSubmit`, `validate`), data fetching | Complex presentational markup |

## Component Rules

- Receive data and callbacks **only via props**
- Name handlers as callbacks: `onLogout`, `onSubmit`, `onChange`
- No imports from `store/`, `services/`, `hooks/` (except type-only imports)

## Container Rules

- Compose the UI component and wire all logic
- Pass handlers and state down as props
- Export as the page default via `index.ts`

## Index Files

Every subdirectory must have an `index.ts` that re-exports its public API. Routes lazy-load the page directory:

```ts
const Dashboard = lazy(() => import("../pages/protected/dashboard"));
```

## Shared / Reusable Code

Before adding helpers, check existing locations:

- `src/utils/` — auth, toasters, formatting
- `src/hooks/` — reusable React hooks
- `src/helpers/` — HTTP helpers
- `src/components/shared/` — app-wide UI components

Move logic here only when used across multiple pages or features.

## Example

```tsx
// components/Login.tsx — UI only
type LoginProps = { onLogin: () => void; loading: boolean };
const Login = ({ onLogin, loading }: LoginProps) => (
  <Button onClick={onLogin} disabled={loading}>Login</Button>
);

// container/LoginContainer.tsx — logic
const LoginContainer = () => {
  const { makeApiCall: login, loading } = useApiRequest(...);
  const handleLogin = async () => { /* ... */ };
  return <Login onLogin={handleLogin} loading={loading} />;
};
```
